# macrdp configuration.
#
# Copy to:  ~/Library/Application Support/macrdp/config.env
# (install-launchagent.sh does this for you on first run if it's missing.)
#
# Plain key=value, read by the signed macrdp binary itself (launchd runs it as
# `macrdp --config <this file>`). Edit it freely — it is NOT inside the signed
# .app bundle, so changes never disturb the TCC grants.
# After editing, apply with:
#     launchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/com.clintcan.macrdp

# Listen address. Use 127.0.0.1:3390 for loopback-only, 0.0.0.0:3390 for LAN.
BIND="127.0.0.1:3390"

# macOS account to authenticate RDP clients against. Defaults to the login user.
# USERNAME="clintcan"

# Read the password from the Keychain so launchd can start without a terminal.
# Store it once with:
#     security add-generic-password -s macrdp -a "$(id -un)" -w 'YOUR_PASSWORD'
USE_KEYCHAIN=1

# TLS certificate. By default macrdp auto-generates a self-signed cert (trust-on-
# first-use) in ~/Library/Application Support/macrdp. To serve a real CA / ACME /
# Let's Encrypt cert so clients can verify the server's identity, point these at
# your PEM files (BOTH required; leaf-first chain in the cert; key chmod 600 and
# readable by the macrdp user). The cert's CN/SAN must match the hostname or IP
# clients connect to, or they'll report a name mismatch. A cert change needs a
# restart (kickstart -k).
# TLS_CERT="/etc/letsencrypt/live/mac.example.com/fullchain.pem"
# TLS_KEY="/etc/letsencrypt/live/mac.example.com/privkey.pem"

# Auth hardening (on by default). macrdp rate-limits and (briefly, escalating)
# locks out source IPs that hammer the port with connection attempts, and writes
# an audit line per connection (accept/reject/disconnect + outcome) to the log.
# Loopback (127.0.0.1/::1) is always exempt, so you can never lock yourself out
# locally — these only matter when BIND exposes the server to other hosts. The
# defaults are conservative and won't bite a normal user; tune or disable only if
# needed. Audit lines are tagged `macrdp::audit` (grep ~/Library/Logs/macrdp.log).
# CONN_GUARD=1               # master switch (0 = disable rate-limit + lockout)
# AUDIT_LOG=1                # connection audit log (independent of CONN_GUARD)
# GUARD_RL_MAX=10            # max attempts per window per IP (0 = no rate-limit)
# GUARD_RL_WINDOW_SECS=60    # rate-limit sliding window
# GUARD_FAIL_THRESHOLD=5     # consecutive failures before lockout (0 = no lockout)
# GUARD_FAILFAST_SECS=3      # only an errored connection that failed within this
#                            # window (never got past the handshake) counts toward
#                            # lockout; a client that connected longer then errored
#                            # is treated as legit (won't lock out reconnecting clients)
# GUARD_COOLDOWN_BASE_SECS=30  # first lockout length, doubles per extra failure:
#                              # 5 fails=30s, 6=60s, 7=120s, 8=240s, 9=480s, 10+=cap.
#                              # Auto-expires (no manual unlock); a clean session resets.
# GUARD_COOLDOWN_MAX_SECS=900  # lockout escalation cap (15 min)

# --- Health-check watchdog (on by default when headless / under launchd) ---
# Detects a hung-but-alive process (deadlocked tokio runtime / all workers
# blocked) and exits so the LaunchAgent restarts a fresh one — KeepAlive alone
# only restarts on an outright crash, not a wedge. Skipped on --fork-workers
# workers and, by default, interactively. Conservative defaults (~90 s to bounce)
# so load spikes never trip it. Rarely need changing.
# HEALTH_CHECK=1                # force on even interactive; 0/off = disable
# HEALTHCHECK_INTERVAL_SECS=15  # delay between liveness probes
# HEALTHCHECK_TIMEOUT_SECS=30   # max time a probe may take before it's a miss
# HEALTHCHECK_FAILURES=2        # consecutive misses before the process exits

# Feature toggles (1 = on, 0 = off).
ENABLE_H264=0
ENABLE_AAC=0
HIDPI=0
# Drive redirection (RDPDR): let the connecting client redirect its local drive
# so the Mac can browse/read the client's files. Read-only; in progress. The
# client must also opt in (mstsc: Local Resources -> Drives).
ENABLE_DRIVE_REDIRECTION=0
# Smart-card redirection (RDPDR / MS-RDPESC): let the connecting client redirect
# its smart-card reader so macOS apps can use the card through it. Requires the
# PC/SC IFD handler to be installed once (packaging/install-ifd-handler.sh, or
# macrdp.app/Contents/Resources/install-ifd-handler.sh) and a USB device present
# to trigger its load. The client must also opt in (mstsc: Local Resources ->
# More -> Smart cards; FreeRDP: /smartcard).
ENABLE_SMARTCARD_REDIRECTION=0
# Cmd+Tab also un-minimizes the target app's window (brings it back from the
# Dock). Off matches native macOS (Cmd+Tab activates a minimized app but leaves
# its window minimized).
UNMINIMIZE=0

# Also accept Option+Tab (Alt+Tab from the client) as an app-switch trigger, in
# addition to Cmd+Tab. Useful for clients that forward Alt+Tab but gate Win+Tab
# (e.g. mstsc windowed). Off = Option+Tab reaches remote apps as a normal key.
ALT_TAB_SWITCH=0

# Show a visual app-switcher overlay (icon row) on the remote during Cmd+Tab /
# Option+Tab, like macOS's native switcher. macrdp spawns a small helper that
# draws a real on-screen panel (captured by ScreenCaptureKit). Off by default.
APP_SWITCHER_HUD=0

# Remap a curated set of Windows editing shortcuts from Ctrl to Cmd so Windows
# muscle memory works: Ctrl+C/V/X/A/Z/S/F/N/T/W/O/P/R/G (and Shift variants, e.g.
# Ctrl+Shift+Z = redo) fire as the Cmd equivalent. Off = Ctrl reaches remote apps
# unchanged (macOS shortcuts need the client's Win/Super key, i.e. Cmd). Cmd+Q is
# never produced (Q excluded); nav keys untouched. Always suppressed when a
# terminal is frontmost so Ctrl+C stays SIGINT.
MAP_CTRL_TO_CMD=0

# Extra bundle ids (comma-separated) where MAP_CTRL_TO_CMD is suppressed, on top
# of the built-in terminal list. Use for apps with an embedded terminal that
# can't be auto-detected (the frontmost app is the IDE, not a TTY). No effect
# unless MAP_CTRL_TO_CMD=1.
# NO_REMAP_APPS="com.microsoft.VSCode,com.todesktop.230313mzl4w4u92"

# Non-US keyboard layout. By DEFAULT macrdp auto-detects the client's layout
# (from the KLID it announces) and translates keys against it — so a French/
# German/etc. client types the right characters with no config (US clients are
# unaffected). The Mac's own input source is never changed. Set this only to
# FORCE a specific layout, or "none" to disable translation entirely. Accepts a
# short name (french, de, azerty, swissgerman, …), a Windows KLID (0x040C), or a
# macOS input-source id (com.apple.keylayout.French). Must be installed on the Mac.
# KEYBOARD_LAYOUT="french"

# Headless virtual display: serve a separate desktop at VD_WIDTH×VD_HEIGHT
# instead of mirroring the panel. On a Mac whose native resolution doesn't match
# the client, this is captured 1:1 (no scaling) and is much snappier than
# mirroring + scaling.
VIRTUAL_DISPLAY=0
# What to do with the physical screen while a client is connected (needs
# VIRTUAL_DISPLAY=1):
#   none    - leave it on; your apps stay on it, the virtual desktop is empty
#   detach  - disable it; macOS moves your apps onto the virtual display so you
#             see and control them remotely (recommended)
#   capture - keep it active but gamma-black it; apps stay on it (use only if
#             'detach' doesn't visibly blank your panel)
PRIMARY_MODE=none
VD_WIDTH=1920
VD_HEIGHT=1080

# EXPERIMENTAL — RDP UDP multitransport (MS-RDPEMT over reliable RDPEUDP). Offer
# an auxiliary UDP transport to clients that advertise it and bind a UDP listener
# on the same address/port as TCP. On its own this keeps EGFX on TCP (a proven
# safe spike) — set UDP_MIGRATE_EGFX=1 too to actually move the H.264 video onto
# the reliable UDP tunnel. Input/audio/clipboard always ride TCP. Mutually
# exclusive with FORK_WORKERS (the UDP socket must outlive reconnects, which a
# per-connection worker can't own — with both set, the client falls back to TCP).
ENABLE_UDP_MULTITRANSPORT=0
# Migrate EGFX (H.264) onto the reliable UDP tunnel via MS-RDPEDYC Soft-Sync
# (needs ENABLE_UDP_MULTITRANSPORT=1 and ENABLE_H264=1). Clean-link optimal: the
# reliable tunnel is an ordered stream, so under packet loss it head-of-line-
# blocks like TCP — but an auto-recovery watchdog detects the wedge (~3s of silent
# EGFX acks) and de-migrates EGFX back to TCP (one-way per session), so the session
# keeps running instead of freezing until reconnect (audio always rode TCP). Best
# on a low-loss link; pair with ADAPTIVE_BITRATE=1 to back off before the wedge.
UDP_MIGRATE_EGFX=0

# Congestion-responsive H.264 rate control on BOTH the UDP tunnel and the TCP path
# (needs ENABLE_H264=1). An AIMD controller reads the client's frame-ack lag
# (EWMA-smoothed) + reliable-tunnel retransmits and live-adjusts the encoder
# bitrate within [floor, --bitrate ceiling]: backs off under congestion, climbs
# back when the link clears (hysteresis + a 3-zone hold so single spikes don't pump
# the bitrate). So --bitrate becomes a CEILING, not a fixed target — set it high
# (e.g. EXTRA_FLAGS="--bitrate 8000000") and let it adapt. Leave 0 for a fixed
# bitrate. Tunables: MACRDP_UDP_ADAPTIVE_{FLOOR_BPS,INCREASE_BPS,DECREASE,
# INTERVAL_MS}, MACRDP_{UDP,TCP}_ADAPTIVE_LAG_THRESHOLD, MACRDP_ADAPTIVE_EWMA_ALPHA.
ADAPTIVE_BITRATE=0

# EXPERIMENTAL — lossy-UDP audio with 1+1 redundancy (the loss-resilient audio path).
# Implies ENABLE_UDP_MULTITRANSPORT=1; needs ENABLE_AAC=1 + ENABLE_H264=1. Streams
# RDPSND audio over a LOSSY UDP/DTLS tunnel: AAC Wave2 data rides a lossy RDPEUDP flow
# (deliver-on-arrival, no retransmit) and each datagram is sent TWICE (the client's DTLS
# anti-replay dedups), so an independent-loss link of rate p drops a payload only at p^2.
# Verified smooth on mstsc at 5/10/15% loss where single-send glitches. One switch —
# replaces the four MACRDP_UDP_{OFFER_FECL,LOSSY_DELIVERY,LOSSY_AUDIO,LOSSY_AUDIO_DUP}
# env gates (which still work standalone as a fallback). Leave 0 for audio over TCP.
ENABLE_LOSSY_AUDIO=0

# EXPERIMENTAL — fork-per-connection workers (xrdp's model). A thin supervisor
# owns the virtual display + headless blanking and spawns a FRESH worker process
# per RDP connection. This makes mstsc reconnect to a still-running server render
# (a fresh process dodges mstsc's retained-EGFX-surface bug) instead of going
# blank; a rare residual blank recovers by reconnecting once more. Supervisor-
# owned vdisplay/blanking persist across reconnects; the physical panel stays
# blanked the whole session and restores on exit. Requires VIRTUAL_DISPLAY=1 for
# the headless modes (mirror-primary also works). Mutually-exclusive flags are
# validated at startup. Leave 0 unless you specifically want reconnect resilience.
FORK_WORKERS=0

# Escape hatch: any other flags, space-separated, e.g.:
#     EXTRA_FLAGS="--fps 60 --bitrate 8000000"
EXTRA_FLAGS=""
